::ENG3_U06_UA_Q01::MLA.ENG3.C.01
Context: A student is planning an oral presentation about a research-based literary claim.
Which plan is strongest?{
~List facts in random order without a claim.#Incorrect. Random facts do not create focused oral communication.
=State the claim, organize evidence logically, and explain how the evidence supports the claim.#Correct. This supports clear oral communication and audience understanding.
~Use only a decorative slide and no evidence.#Incorrect. The audience needs credible evidence and explanation.
~Begin with a new topic not connected to the research.#Incorrect. An unrelated topic weakens focus.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q02::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A digital slide contains a timeline of events from a text.
What should the presenter add?{
~A random animation after each date.#Incorrect. Random animation may distract from the timeline.
~More dates that do not connect to the claim.#Incorrect. Irrelevant dates can overwhelm the audience.
=A caption explaining how the timeline supports the claim.#Correct. A caption connects multimedia to audience understanding.
~A background that makes the dates hard to read.#Incorrect. Readability matters in digital presentation.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q03::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student prepares a paragraph for online publication.
Which choice best shows publication readiness?{
=The paragraph is revised for audience, clarity, evidence, and conventions.#Correct. Quality writing must be prepared for readers before publication.
~The paragraph is posted without proofreading.#Incorrect. Publication requires checking clarity and conventions.
~The paragraph has no audience in mind.#Incorrect. Audience fit is central to publication.
~The paragraph replaces explanation with decoration.#Incorrect. Decoration does not replace writing quality.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q04::MLA.ENG3.RS.01
Context: A student uses research to support a presentation.
Which use is most responsible?{
~Use research without naming the source.#Incorrect. Source responsibility supports credibility.
~Use the source only because it is easy to find.#Incorrect. Ease does not prove relevance or credibility.
~Include source details but no connection to the claim.#Incorrect. Research must be connected to the message.
=Use relevant source information and explain how it supports the claim.#Correct. Research should be relevant, credible, and synthesized with the presentation purpose.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q05::MLA.ENG3.W.04
Context: A draft sentence says, "This proves stuff about the topic."
Which revision is strongest?{
~This proves a lot about many things.#Incorrect. The wording is still vague.
=The character's response to conflict supports the claim because it shows how pressure reveals personal values.#Correct. This revision is clearer, more specific, and better organized.
~This proves stuff because it is important.#Incorrect. This repeats vague language.
~The topic has stuff that matters.#Incorrect. This does not explain evidence or reasoning.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q06::MLA.ENG3.C.01
Context: A speaker wants to use a rhetorical question.
When is it effective?{
=When it focuses the audience on the central claim.#Correct. A rhetorical question can guide attention when it supports the presentation purpose.
~When it introduces an unrelated issue.#Incorrect. Unrelated questions distract from the claim.
~When it replaces all evidence.#Incorrect. Rhetorical devices do not replace evidence.
~When it confuses the audience.#Incorrect. Rhetorical choices should improve communication.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q07::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student includes a photograph in a digital presentation.
Which question best tests its usefulness?{
~Does it fill empty space?#Incorrect. Filling space is not the purpose of multimedia.
~Is it the brightest image available?#Incorrect. Brightness does not prove relevance.
=Does it help the audience understand the claim or evidence?#Correct. Multimedia should improve audience understanding.
~Can it replace the entire presentation?#Incorrect. Multimedia supports communication but does not replace the message.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q08::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student publishes a page for classmates who have not read the source text.
What should the student include?{
~Only unexplained quotations.#Incorrect. Readers need context and explanation.
=Brief context, a clear claim, evidence, and explanation.#Correct. This helps the intended audience understand the writing.
~A file with no title.#Incorrect. A title helps readers understand the purpose.
~Only decorative media.#Incorrect. Decoration does not communicate the claim.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q09::MLA.ENG3.V.01
Context: A student explains a multimedia choice using academic vocabulary.
Which sentence is strongest?{
~This image is good because it looks nice.#Incorrect. The wording is vague and not analytical.
=This image clarifies the historical context that shapes the character's conflict.#Correct. The vocabulary is precise and connected to analysis.
~This picture has some stuff from the story.#Incorrect. The wording is vague.
~This visual is cool and dramatic.#Incorrect. This focuses on appearance instead of meaning.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q10::MLA.ENG3.L.01
Context: A published caption contains several spelling errors.
What is the best correction step?{
=Proofread and correct the caption before publishing.#Correct. Standard English conventions support clarity and credibility.
~Leave the errors because captions are optional.#Incorrect. Visible text should be accurate.
~Delete all captions even if they explain media.#Incorrect. Captions can be useful when corrected.
~Ignore the errors because the page is digital.#Incorrect. Digital publication still requires conventions.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q11::MLA.ENG3.C.01
Context: A speaker has credible evidence but no organization.
What should be revised?{
~The topic should become unrelated.#Incorrect. The topic should remain focused.
=The order of ideas so the audience can follow the claim, evidence, and explanation.#Correct. Organization is essential for oral communication.
~The evidence should be hidden.#Incorrect. Evidence should be presented clearly.
~The conclusion should introduce a new claim.#Incorrect. A conclusion should reinforce the focus.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q12::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A slide has a graph with tiny labels.
Which revision improves the audience experience?{
~Make the labels even smaller.#Incorrect. Smaller labels reduce readability.
~Remove the title and source note.#Incorrect. Titles and source notes help readers.
=Simplify the graph and label the details connected to the claim.#Correct. This supports audience understanding.
~Add unrelated clip art beside the graph.#Incorrect. Unrelated media distracts from the graph.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q13::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student says the product is ready because it has a picture.
What is the best feedback?{
~A picture is the only publication requirement.#Incorrect. Publication requires quality writing and audience fit.
~The writing no longer matters.#Incorrect. Writing remains central to publication.
=Check whether the writing is revised and whether the picture supports understanding.#Correct. Both writing quality and multimedia purpose matter.
~Publish without reviewing sources.#Incorrect. Source checks support credibility.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q14::MLA.ENG3.RS.01
Context: A student combines primary text evidence with secondary research.
What should the student do?{
~List both sources without explaining either one.#Incorrect. Listing sources is not synthesis.
=Explain how both sources work together to support the interpretation.#Correct. Synthesis connects sources to an interpretation.
~Use only the secondary source and ignore the text.#Incorrect. Literary research should connect primary and secondary sources.
~Use unrelated research because it sounds advanced.#Incorrect. Research must be relevant.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q15::MLA.ENG3.W.04
Context: Feedback says a paragraph is too broad for publication.
Which revision is best?{
~Add more vague statements.#Incorrect. Vague statements increase the problem.
=Focus the paragraph on one claim and remove unrelated details.#Correct. This improves clarity and structure.
~Remove all evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence supports the claim.
~Change only the font.#Incorrect. Font does not fix broad content.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q16::MLA.ENG3.C.01
Context: A speaker wants to build credibility.
Which choice helps most?{
=Use accurate evidence and explain the source's relevance.#Correct. Credible evidence and explanation build trust.
~Use claims without support.#Incorrect. Unsupported claims weaken credibility.
~Distract the audience with unrelated humor.#Incorrect. Unrelated humor does not build credibility.
~Avoid explaining the source.#Incorrect. The audience needs to understand why evidence matters.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q17::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student uses a video clip in a digital presentation.
When is the clip appropriate?{
~When it is unrelated but entertaining.#Incorrect. Entertainment without relevance distracts.
~When it is too long for the presentation purpose.#Incorrect. Overlong clips can weaken focus.
=When it directly clarifies a point the audience needs to understand.#Correct. Purposeful media improves audience experience.
~When it replaces all analysis.#Incorrect. Media supports analysis but does not replace it.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q18::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student is ready to export a publication file.
Which final check is best?{
~Check only whether the page has color.#Incorrect. Color is not enough.
=Check audience fit, readable formatting, source information, multimedia, and conventions.#Correct. These checks support publication readiness.
~Check whether the file avoids all writing.#Incorrect. Quality writing is required.
~Check whether every detail from notes is included.#Incorrect. Too much detail may overwhelm readers.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q19::MLA.ENG3.V.01
Context: A presentation uses the term "synthesis."
What should the speaker do if the audience may not know the word?{
~Use the word repeatedly without explanation.#Incorrect. Repetition alone may not support understanding.
=Briefly define it and show how it applies to the evidence.#Correct. Academic vocabulary should be clear and useful.
~Avoid the concept completely.#Incorrect. The concept can be used if explained.
~Replace all evidence with the vocabulary word.#Incorrect. Vocabulary does not replace evidence.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q20::MLA.ENG3.L.01
Context: A slide title has incorrect capitalization.
Why should it be revised?{
~Titles never matter in presentations.#Incorrect. Titles guide audience understanding.
~Capitalization errors make the work more personal.#Incorrect. Errors weaken polish and clarity.
=Correct capitalization supports professionalism and readability.#Correct. Conventions matter in presentation and publication materials.
~The title should be removed from every slide.#Incorrect. Titles can help organize information.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q21::MLA.ENG3.C.01
Context: A speaker's conclusion introduces a brand-new claim.
What is the issue?{
=The conclusion may confuse the audience instead of reinforcing the presentation's focus.#Correct. A conclusion should synthesize and reinforce, not start a new topic.
~The conclusion is automatically stronger with a new claim.#Incorrect. New claims can weaken closure.
~The audience does not need focus.#Incorrect. Focus is essential.
~The evidence becomes unnecessary.#Incorrect. Evidence remains important.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q22::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student has two possible images for a slide.
Which image should be chosen?{
~The one that is unrelated but dramatic.#Incorrect. Drama does not replace relevance.
~The one that fills the most space.#Incorrect. Size alone does not support understanding.
=The one that best supports the point and can be explained clearly.#Correct. Purpose and explanation make media effective.
~The one with no connection to the audience.#Incorrect. Audience understanding matters.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q23::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A LAN publication has no clear audience.
What should the writer do?{
~Publish anyway because anyone might read it.#Incorrect. A specific audience guides revision.
=Define the audience and revise tone, context, and media choices for that group.#Correct. Audience tailoring is required for publication readiness.
~Remove all context from the writing.#Incorrect. Context often helps the audience.
~Add unrelated media to make the audience broader.#Incorrect. Unrelated media does not solve audience drift.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q24::MLA.ENG3.W.04
Context: A student revises a product after feedback.
Which change best reflects meaningful revision?{
=Clarifying the explanation and connecting the multimedia to the claim.#Correct. This improves structure, clarity, and integration.
~Changing only the background pattern.#Incorrect. Surface design does not address content quality.
~Deleting all evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is needed.
~Adding unrelated paragraphs.#Incorrect. Unrelated additions weaken focus.
}

::ENG3_U06_UA_Q25::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student describes mastery for Unit 6.
Which statement is strongest?{
~I can decorate slides and skip writing revision.#Incorrect. Unit 6 requires communication, writing quality, and publication readiness.
~I can publish any draft without checking the audience.#Incorrect. Audience fit is required.
~I can use media without explaining it.#Incorrect. Media must support understanding.
=I can communicate a research-based message through clear speaking, purposeful digital media, revised writing, and publication checks.#Correct. This synthesizes the Unit 6 standards.
}
